  <rules>
    <rule>"Funding" means Transaction.type_name = "Incoming Funds". The funder is the Organisation on the PROVIDES relationship; leave out rows where that Organisation is the activity's own reporting organisation. Never add different transaction types together: WFP repeats every Outgoing Commitment as an identical Expenditure.</rule>
    <rule>Money: for exact totals use sum(value_cents) / 100.0 (dividing by the integer 100 truncates). Report positive and negative transactions separately (amount, adjustments, net = amount + adjustments). Group by currency; never add or convert currencies.</rule>
    <rule>Dates are DATE values: use t.date.year, not string comparison. Activity.start_date and end_date hold the actual date when there is one, otherwise the planned date.</rule>
    <rule>Citations: every node has source_id and every relationship has source_ids (a list), each naming a source CSV row, for example "nodes_transactions.csv:row:12".</rule>
    <rule>An Organisation.ref starting with "name:" has no IATI identifier. One organisation can appear under several refs, so resolve names with resolve_entity or the retrieval tools, not by matching Organisation.name in Cypher.</rule>
    <rule>The graph holds only hunger-related activities in the hotspot countries, so totals are not totals for a publisher as a whole.</rule>
    <rule>A value list written as "code=name" (for example "1=Incoming Funds") means the property holds the code ("1"); the property with the same name plus "_name" holds the name ("Incoming Funds").</rule>
  </rules>
